Bring both conditions onto the single vector \(\vec{b} - \vec{c}\). From \(\vec{a} \cdot \vec{b} = \vec{a} \cdot \vec{c}\) we get \(\vec{a} \cdot (\vec{b} - \vec{c}) = 0\), and from \(\vec{a} 	imes \vec{b} = \vec{a} 	imes \vec{c}\) we get \(\vec{a} 	imes (\vec{b} - \vec{c}) = \vec{0}\). Suppose, for contradiction, that \(\vec{b} 
eq \vec{c}\), so \(\vec{b} - \vec{c}\) is a non-zero vector. Since \(\vec{a} 
eq \vec{0}\) as well, the first equation forces \(\vec{a}\) to be perpendicular to \(\vec{b} - \vec{c}\), while the second forces \(\vec{a}\) to be parallel to it. A non-zero vector cannot be both: if \(\vec{a}\) is parallel to \(\vec{b} - \vec{c}\) then \(\vec{a} = k(\vec{b} - \vec{c})\) for some scalar \(k 
eq 0\), and then \(\vec{a} \cdot (\vec{b} - \vec{c}) = k\,|\vec{b} - \vec{c}|^2 
eq 0\), contradicting the first equation. So the supposition is untenable, \(\vec{b} - \vec{c} = \vec{0}\), and therefore \(\vec{b} = \vec{c}\). Note that both hypotheses are needed: the dot condition alone leaves \(\vec{b}\) and \(\vec{c}\) free to differ by any vector perpendicular to \(\vec{a}\), and the cross condition alone leaves them free to differ by any multiple of \(\vec{a}\).
